The program prints several OpenMP runtime parameters before entering the parallel region:

omp_get_num_procs() returns the number of CPU cores available to the process.
This value corresponds to the number of CPUs allocated through Slurm via cpus-per-task.

omp_get_max_threads() returns the maximum number of OpenMP threads that the program may spawn.


Inside the parallel region, every thread prints:
its thread ID (omp_get_thread_num()), the total number of threads in the parallel region (omp_get_num_threads()), 
and the process ID (getpid()), which allows us to distinguish between multiple processes when ntasks-per-node = 2.


The final output contains multiple lines printed by different threads and possibly multiple processes. The lines appear in no particular order because:

OpenMP threads run concurrently.
There is no synchronization around the printf call, so threads print whenever they reach that point.

Multiple processes run concurrently when ntasks-per-node = 2.
Both processes write to the same output file at the same time, interleaving their output unpredictably.

The operating system’s scheduler determines execution order.
Print statements compete for shared stdout, resulting in nondeterministic ordering.
